##Use the provided Nextess logo video as a normal frontend asset.

Prefer a structure similar to:

frontend-v2/
├── public/
│   └── branding/
│       └── nextess-loading.mp4

The exact location may be adapted to the existing repository structure if there is already an established asset convention.

IMPORTANT:

The video must be referenced through a centralized configuration or reusable component rather than hardcoded throughout multiple pages.

For example:

const NEXTESS_LOADING_VIDEO = "/branding/nextess-loading.mp4";

or an equivalent existing project convention.

This is important because the video may be replaced in the future.

If the file name/path changes, there should ideally be only one place that needs to be updated.

Do Not Build Around a Fixed Timer

Do NOT simply do:

show video for 3 seconds
→ hide video
→ show application

The loading screen must be controlled primarily by actual application readiness.

The video may finish before the application is ready, or the application may become ready before the video finishes.

Handle both cases gracefully.

Conceptually:

Loading screen visible
        ↓
Video plays
        +
Application initializes
        ↓
Wait until required initialization is complete
        ↓
Transition to application
Do not artificially delay the application just to make the video play longer than necessary.

##Initial Application Load
When the user opens Nextess for the first time:
Nextess logo/video
        ↓
Initialize application
        ↓
Restore/create anonymous session if required
        ↓
Initialize required frontend state
        ↓
Load essential application configuration/data
        ↓
Application ready
        ↓
Fade/transition into Nextess
This must work with the existing Nextess anonymous exploration flow.
The user should not be forced to log in merely because the loading screen is being displayed.
The current product flow remains:
Open Nextess
→ Start exploring
→ Experience UI/UX
→ Try projects/missions
→ Decide whether to create an account

Do not introduce a login gate.

## Refresh Behavior

When the user refreshes the Nextess application, the branded loading experience should appear again while the application is initializing.

The refresh experience should:

display the Nextess logo/video
initialize the application normally
restore the appropriate anonymous/authenticated session
restore necessary application state
transition smoothly back to the application

Do not reset legitimate user progress merely because the page was refreshed.

## Longer Loading Operations

Where appropriate, create the loading component so it can also support longer application operations.

Examples:

Loading mission...
Preparing investigation...
Loading simulation...
Preparing your workspace...
Almost ready...

Use actual loading state rather than fake progress.

The component should be reusable rather than creating separate implementations for every page.

For example:

<NextessLoadingScreen
    message="Preparing your investigation..."
/>

The exact API should follow the existing frontend architecture.

## User-Facing Messaging

The loading experience should reassure the user that Nextess is working.

Avoid wording such as:

DO NOT CLOSE THE APP
WAIT
PLEASE DON'T LEAVE

unless technically necessary.

Prefer short, positive messages such as:

Getting Nextess ready...
Preparing your workspace...
Loading your mission...
Preparing your investigation...
Almost ready...

The message should not imply that the application is broken or stuck.

Do not unnecessarily display a long message while the logo animation is playing.

## Reusable Component

Create one reusable loading-screen component rather than duplicating loading UI throughout the application.

It should be capable of handling:

initial application loading
refresh/startup
mission loading
other legitimate longer frontend loading states where appropriate

Keep the component independent from specific mission IDs, subjects, projects, or pages.

For example:

NextessLoadingScreen
        ↓
Reusable by
├── Application bootstrap
├── Mission loading
├── Simulation loading
└── Other legitimate loading states

Do not create separate hardcoded loading screens for Physics, Economics, or individual missions.

## Video Behavior

Implement the video in a way that works reliably across modern browsers.

Consider:

autoplay restrictions
muted playback
playsInline
responsive sizing
graceful fallback if video playback fails
accessibility
reduced-motion preferences where appropriate

The loading screen must still function if the browser cannot autoplay or render the video.

The application must never become permanently blocked because the video failed to load.

If the video cannot play, provide an appropriate static/logo fallback and continue application initialization.

## Performance

The loading experience must not unnecessarily make Nextess slower.

Optimize appropriately:

avoid loading the video repeatedly for every small API request
do not block the application indefinitely because of the video
avoid huge uncompressed video files
keep the loading component lightweight
use the appropriate video format/encoding supported by the project
preload only when justified by the startup experience

Do not introduce a large JavaScript dependency merely to display the video.

## Smooth Transition

When the application becomes ready, transition from the loading screen into the main UI smoothly.

For example:

Logo/loading screen
       ↓
short fade
       ↓
Nextess application

Avoid abrupt removal that causes visual flashing.

The transition should also avoid exposing an uninitialized/blank application underneath the loading screen.

## Architecture Integration

Before implementing anything:

Inspect the existing frontend architecture.
Inspect existing routing.
Inspect existing application bootstrap/startup logic.
Inspect existing loading states.
Inspect existing asset conventions.
Inspect existing API/session initialization.
Reuse existing mechanisms where appropriate.
Do not create a parallel application initialization architecture if one already exists.

Integrate the loading experience into the existing Nextess frontend rather than rebuilding the application's startup system.

## Future Video Replacement

The logo video is expected to change in the future.

Design the implementation so that replacing:

nextess-loading.mp4

with another compatible video does not require rewriting React components or application logic.

Ideally the replacement should only require replacing the asset while preserving the same asset reference.

If configuration is necessary, centralize it.

Do NOT embed the binary video data directly into JavaScript/TypeScript source code.

## Do Not Hardcode Application Content

Do not hardcode:

mission IDs
subject IDs
project IDs
page-specific loading logic
individual mission names
Physics/Economics-specific loading components

The loading system must remain generic and reusable as Nextess grows.

## Accessibility

Ensure the loading experience does not create accessibility problems.

Consider:

appropriate accessible text
reduced-motion preferences
keyboard usability
video playback behavior
users who cannot see the animation
users with slow connections

If reduced motion is requested, provide a simpler static logo/loading experience instead of forcing the animated video.

## Error/Fallback Behavior

The application must never remain stuck on the loading screen because of:

video loading failure
video playback failure
missing optional branding asset
temporary media/network issue

Application readiness and branding-media availability must be treated separately.

If the video fails:

Video failure
↓
Static Nextess logo/fallback
↓
Continue application initialization
↓
Open application normally
